Document (dev.stxt.website): STXT Transform: export, import and fill of Slots

	Metadata:
		Status: Genesis
		Last modif: 2026-10-25
		Description: STXT-TRANSFORM-SPEC: how an STXT document is exported to JSON and how it is imported from JSON, driven by its schema, and how the Slots of a document are filled with data.

	Header: @STXT@ Transform

	Subheader: 1. Introduction

	Content >>
		This document is **STXT-TRANSFORM-SPEC**; the other specifications cite it by that name.

		This document defines **@STXT@ Transform**: the operations that take the content of an
		@STXT@ document to JSON, bring it back from JSON and complete it with external data. There are three:

		| Operation  | Input                                        | Output                                 |
		|------------|----------------------------------------------|----------------------------------------|
		| **Export** | An @STXT@ document                           | The same content in JSON               |
		| **Import** | A JSON document                              | The same content as an @STXT@ document |
		| **Fill**   | An @STXT@ document with Slots, and JSON data | A complete @STXT@ document             |

		A **Slot** is the place of a document that waits for a piece of data. Fill puts in every
		Slot the data that belongs to it.

		Its purpose is that an @STXT@ document can be the source of the programs that already
		read JSON, and that the data already existing in JSON can move to @STXT@, without writing
		a conversion of its own for every namespace.

		The three operations are defined on **JSON**: export produces it, import reads it and
		fill takes its data from it. The formats that represent the same data (objects, lists and values),
		such as YAML or NestedText, go through JSON with their own tools: this specification
		does not deal with them, and an implementation does not need to read or write them. Node
		formats, such as XML or KDL, have no single correspondence with JSON and will be dealt
		with in sections of their own, probably for export only: writing them from the document
		and its definition is simple, and reading them needs a parser for each format. Export is
		enough for the intended use: writing in @STXT@ and taking the content as input to
		programs that read other formats.

		Export and import rely on the **definition** of the namespace of the document, whether
		a schema or a template. The text of a document does not say which value is a number,
		which node is a list or how its elements are named: its definition does. That is why
		these two operations need little more information than the document and its
		definition, and why this specification requires for them, for now, that the document
		has a namespace and that the namespace has a definition.

		Fill needs neither a definition nor a namespace: what every Slot does is written in
		the document itself.

		This specification changes neither the syntax of [*STXT-SPEC*](stxt-core-ref) nor the
		validation of [*STXT-SCHEMA-SPEC*](stxt-schema-ref): it is a layer that comes after
		parsing and validation, and optional like them.

	Subsubheader: 1.1 Date and status of this specification

	Content >>
		This specification carries its own **date** and its own **status** in the `Last modif` and
		`Status` fields of its `Metadata`, **independent** of the other @STXT@ specifications' and with
		the meaning fixed by [*STXT-SPEC* §1.1](stxt-core-ref#s1-1). It is in `Genesis`: under
		construction, and any part of it may change without notice.
		It depends on [*STXT-SPEC*](stxt-core-ref) and [*STXT-SCHEMA-SPEC*](stxt-schema-ref).

		Today it holds the presentation of the three operations and two examples: export and
		import with JSON, and a first sketch of the fill of Slots. The rules of JSON and
		those of Slots are still to be written.

	Subsubheader: 1.2 Relation to the canonical tree

	Content >>
		[*STXT-TREE-SPEC*](stxt-tree-ref) already defines a JSON representation of a document:
		the canonical tree. Its purpose is a different one. The canonical tree describes the
		result of parsing, with every node as an object that carries its name, its canonical
		name, its namespace, its form and its value, and it exists to compare implementations.
		It needs no definition, and every value is text.

		The export of this specification produces the JSON that someone designing that data
		directly in JSON would write: objects with one key per node, arrays, numbers and
		booleans. It needs the definition, and it is the form meant to be consumed by a
		program.

	Subsubheader: 1.3 Relation to templates

	Content >>
		A document with Slots is not a `@stxt.template`. A template
		([*STXT-TEMPLATE-SPEC*](stxt-template-ref)) describes the structure of **every**
		document of a namespace and is used to validate them. A **document with Slots** is
		**one** document, with or without a namespace, with values missing: its Slots are filled with
		external data, for example a JSON text, and the result is a complete document, which
		is validated like any other.

		To avoid the confusion, this specification never calls the document with Slots a
		"template".

	Subheader: 2. Terminology

	Content >>
		The keywords **"MUST"**, **"MUST NOT"**, **"SHOULD"**, **"SHOULD NOT"**, and **"MAY"** must be interpreted according to **RFC 2119** and **RFC 8174**: they carry that meaning only when they appear in uppercase, as here.

		Terms such as *node*, *namespace*, *schema*, *template*, *type* and *cardinality* retain their
		meaning in [*STXT-SPEC*](stxt-core-ref), [*STXT-SCHEMA-SPEC*](stxt-schema-ref) and
		[*STXT-TEMPLATE-SPEC*](stxt-template-ref).

		Additional definitions:

		* **Definition**: the schema or the template of the namespace of a document, as in [*STXT-DISCOVERY-SPEC*](stxt-discovery-ref).
		* **Export**: the operation that produces, from an @STXT@ document and its definition, a JSON document.
		* **Import**: the operation that produces, from a JSON document and a definition, an @STXT@ document.
		* **Slot**: the place of an @STXT@ document that fill replaces with a piece of data.
		* **Document with Slots**: an @STXT@ document that contains one or more Slots.
		* **Fill**: the operation that produces a complete @STXT@ document from a document with Slots and some data.

	Subheader: 3. An example: @STXT@ and JSON

	Content >>
		This section is not normative. It shows the direction of export and import with a
		simple document.

		A document:

	Code >>
		# The web server of the office
		Server (com.example.servers):
			Name: web-01
			Active: true
			Ports:
				Port: 80
				Port: 443
			Owner:
				Person: Ana Ruiz
				Email: ana@example.com
			Notes >>
				Restart after every deployment.
				Do not switch off during office hours.

	Content >>
		And the definition of its namespace, here a template:

	Code >>
		Template (@stxt.template): com.example.servers
			Structure >>
				Server (com.example.servers): GROUP
					Name: (1)
					Active: (1) BOOLEAN
					Ports: (1) GROUP
						Port: (+) NATURAL
					Owner: (?) GROUP
						Person: (1)
						Email: (1) EMAIL
					Notes: (?) TEXT

	Subsubheader: 3.1 Export

	Content >>
		The export of the document to JSON:

	Json >>
		{
			"Name": "web-01",
			"Active": true,
			"Ports": [80, 443],
			"Owner": {
				"Person": "Ana Ruiz",
				"Email": "ana@example.com"
			},
			"Notes": "Restart after every deployment.\nDo not switch off during office hours."
		}

	Content >>
		What the definition decides:

		| In the document                 | In the definition                            | In JSON                                         |
		|---------------------------------|----------------------------------------------|-------------------------------------------------|
		| `Name: web-01`                  | No type                                      | A string                                        |
		| `Active: true`                  | `BOOLEAN`                                    | A boolean                                       |
		| `Port: 80`                      | `NATURAL`                                    | A number                                        |
		| `Ports`, with two `Port`        | `GROUP` with a single child, which repeats   | An array. The name `Port` does not appear       |
		| `Owner`, with two children      | `GROUP` with different children              | An object, with one key per child               |
		| `Notes >>`                      | `TEXT`                                       | A string, with the lines separated by `\n`      |

		* Without a definition, `80` and `true` would be text, and nothing would tell a list from a node with a single child.
		* The comment is not exported: it is not part of the tree of the document.
		* The root node does not appear: the JSON is the content of `Server`.

		By contrast, the `Ports` node in the canonical tree of
		[*STXT-TREE-SPEC*](stxt-tree-ref), which is `[80, 443]` in the export:

	Json >>
		{
			"name": "Ports",
			"canonicalName": "ports",
			"namespace": "com.example.servers",
			"form": "inline",
			"value": "",
			"children": [
				{
					"name": "Port",
					"canonicalName": "port",
					"namespace": "com.example.servers",
					"form": "inline",
					"value": "80",
					"children": []
				},
				{
					"name": "Port",
					"canonicalName": "port",
					"namespace": "com.example.servers",
					"form": "inline",
					"value": "443",
					"children": []
				}
			]
		}

	Subsubheader: 3.2 Import

	Content >>
		Import is the way back. It receives the JSON above and the root node it must produce,
		`Server (com.example.servers)`, and returns this document:

	Code >>
		Server (com.example.servers):
			Name: web-01
			Active: true
			Ports:
				Port: 80
				Port: 443
			Owner:
				Person: Ana Ruiz
				Email: ana@example.com
			Notes >>
				Restart after every deployment.
				Do not switch off during office hours.

	Content >>
		The definition supplies what the JSON does not carry:

		* The name `Port` of every element of the `Ports` array.
		* The namespace of every node.
		* The form of every node. If the definition only allows one, it is that one. If it allows both, as `TEXT` does, the one that loses less is chosen: a block if the text has line breaks or starts with blanks, which an inline value would trim; inline otherwise. `Notes` is written as a `>>` block because its text has more than one line.

		Import accepts a string, a number or a boolean as the value of any node with no children,
		whatever its type in the definition, and writes it as text: `Port` gets the same from `80`
		as from `"80"`. That way the data of formats with no types, such as NestedText, where
		every value is a string, is imported too. What does not fit its type, such as `"abc"` in
		`Port`, is rejected by the validation of the imported document.

		The imported document has the same tree as the original. Only the comment has been
		lost.

	Subheader: 4. An example: fill of Slots

	Content >>
		This section is not normative. It is a first sketch of Slots and of how they combine.

		A document with Slots:

	Code >>
		Order (com.example.orders): <number>
			Date: <date>
			Currency: EUR
			Customer: {customer}
				Name: <name>
				Email: <email>
				# The phone is optional
				Phone: ?<phone>
			Line: [lines]<product>
				Quantity: <quantity>
				Price: <price>
				Note: ?<note>
			Shipment: [shipments]
				Carrier: <carrier>
				Reference: ${number}/${carrier}
				Parcel: [parcels]
			Instructions >>
				Order ${number}, dated ${date}.
				Deliver in the morning.
			Label >>
				[shipments]
				Order ${number}
				Carrier: ${carrier}

	Content >>
		The data, in JSON:

	Json >>
		{
			"number": "2026-0142",
			"date": "2026-10-12",
			"customer": {
				"name": "Athena Bookshop",
				"email": "orders@athena.example"
			},
			"lines": [
				{ "product": "A5 notebook", "quantity": 40, "price": 3.25, "note": "Blue cover" },
				{ "product": "HB pencil", "quantity": 200, "price": 0.45 }
			],
			"shipments": [
				{ "carrier": "Royal Mail", "parcels": ["R-01", "R-02"] },
				{ "carrier": "DHL", "parcels": ["D-07"] }
			]
		}

	Content >>
		And the document that fill produces:

	Code >>
		Order (com.example.orders): 2026-0142
			Date: 2026-10-12
			Currency: EUR
			Customer:
				Name: Athena Bookshop
				Email: orders@athena.example
			Line: A5 notebook
				Quantity: 40
				Price: 3.25
				Note: Blue cover
			Line: HB pencil
				Quantity: 200
				Price: 0.45
			Shipment:
				Carrier: Royal Mail
				Reference: 2026-0142/Royal Mail
				Parcel: R-01
				Parcel: R-02
			Shipment:
				Carrier: DHL
				Reference: 2026-0142/DHL
				Parcel: D-07
			Instructions >>
				Order 2026-0142, dated 2026-10-12.
				Deliver in the morning.
			Label >>
				Order 2026-0142
				Carrier: Royal Mail
			Label >>
				Order 2026-0142
				Carrier: DHL

	Content >>
		The Slots of the example:

		| Slot       | What it does                                                                              | In the example                    |
		|------------|-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|-----------------------------------|
		| `[list]`   | Repeats the node, with its children, once for every element of the array `list`          | `Line`, `Shipment`, `Parcel`      |
		| `{object}` | Enters the object `object`: the Slots of the node and of its children are looked up in it | `Customer: {customer}`            |
		| `<var>`    | Puts the data item `var` as the value of the node; it closes the value                    | `Date: <date>`                    |
		| `${var}`   | Puts the data item `var` inside a text                                                    | `Reference: ${number}/${carrier}` |
		| `?`        | Makes the Slot optional: if the data item does not exist, `?[list]`, `?{object}` and `?<var>` remove the node, and `${?var}` puts nothing | `Phone: ?<phone>` |

		* The name of a Slot can only have ASCII letters, digits, `-` and `_`, and at least one character: `<number>`, `[lines]`, `{customer-data}`, `${id_2}`.
		* The name of a Slot is looked up as it is among the keys of the data, case-sensitively: `<Date>` does not find `date`.
		* The value of a node with Slots has two parts. At the start goes the **navigation**: `[list]` and `{object}`, as many as needed and in any order, and at most one `<var>` at the end. They apply from left to right, and each one repeats or enters over what the previous one leaves: `Address: {customer}[addresses]` enters the customer and repeats the node for each of their addresses. There can be blanks between them and before the text: `{customer} [addresses]` is the same as `{customer}[addresses]`. What follows is text.
		* `<var>` puts the data item as the value of the node and closes the value: only blanks can follow it. `Line: [lines]<product>` repeats the node and puts in each one its `product`. `Line: [lines]<product> (units)` and `Line: <product>[lines]` are errors. The node can have children: `Order: <number>` has `Date`, `Customer` and the rest.
		* In the text, `${var}` puts a data item, and a value can carry several: `Reference: ${number}/${carrier}` produces `Reference: 2026-0142/Royal Mail`. The text around them is copied as it is. `Date: ${date}` produces the same as `Date: <date>`.
		* In the text, `<x>`, `[x]` and `{x}` are text: nothing is repeated, entered or put there any more. `Label: See [note]` is copied as it is, and in a block with HTML or XML `<p>` and `<br>` are text.
		* Everything that has the form `${name}` is a Slot, also in a block: `echo "${HOME}"` expects a data item `HOME`. To write `${` as text, write `$${`: `echo "$${HOME}"` produces `echo "${HOME}"`. It is the only escape. A `$$` not followed by `{` is copied as it is, and what does not have the form of a Slot, such as `${}` or `${a b}`, is text.
		* If the element of an array is an object, fill enters it: every `Shipment` looks up its `carrier` and its `parcels` in its own element.
		* If the element is a value, it is the value of the node: `Parcel: [parcels]` produces `Parcel: R-01` and `Parcel: R-02`. This only holds in a node that has nothing but the navigation: no text, no children, no more block lines. If `parcels` brings values, `Parcel: [parcels] (box)` is an error, and so is `Parcel: [parcels]` with a child `Weight: <weight>`. No further navigation can follow it either: `Parcel: [parcels]{size}` and `Parcel: [parcels]<weight>` are errors.
		* An array that mixes values and objects is an error, and so is an array inside another array. A `null` element is as if it were not there: it produces no node.
		* A Slot looks up its data item first in the current element and, if it is not there, goes up through the context: the object or element that contains it, and so on up to the root of the data. Every `Shipment` finds `carrier` in its element and `number` in the root.
		* A data item whose value is `null` counts as absent: the lookup keeps going up.
		* In a `>>` block, the first line plays the role of the start of the value. If it starts with `[list]`, `{object}` or `<var>`, it is the navigation of the node: it applies as in an inline node and does not pass to the filled document. `Label >>` starts with `[shipments]` and produces one block per shipment. What follows is text.
		* In a block, `<var>` puts the data item as the content of the block and closes the value, as in an inline node: the block cannot have more lines. A `Notes >>` block whose only line is `?<notes>` puts the notes, with their line breaks, or removes the node if there are no notes. `Notes >>` with the line `${notes}` produces the same when there is a data item.
		* A first block line that starts with navigation cannot have anything else: either it is valid navigation as a whole, or it is an error. `[shipments] Order`, `<number> Order`, `{customer} ${name}` and `[looks]{like-navigation} x` are errors, and so is a Markdown link at the start of the block, such as `[Home](index)`.
		* For the same reason, an HTML or XML block cannot start with a tag that has the form of a Slot: a first line `<html>` or `<p>Hello</p>` is an error. It can start with `<!DOCTYPE html>` or `<html lang="en">`, which do not have the form of a Slot.
		* If the first line of a block starts with anything else, the whole block is text, including the `[list]`, `{object}` and `<var>` of any of its lines: `See <b>[note]</b>` is text.
		* In the text of a block, each `${var}` is replaced by its data item and the rest is copied as it is. Inside the block, `Carrier: ${carrier}` is text, not a node.
		* The filled node has the form it has in the document with Slots: `Node: <var>` comes out inline and `Node >>` comes out as a block. A data item with line breaks in an inline node is an error. In a block, it produces several lines.
		* The filled document has the tree its text would give when parsed, with the values normalized as in any document: an inline value with no blanks at the start or the end ([*STXT-SPEC* §5.1](stxt-core-ref#s5-1)), and a block with no blanks at the end of its lines and no empty lines at the end ([§10.2](stxt-core-ref#s10-2) and [§10.3](stxt-core-ref#s10-3)). This applies alike to what the data puts and to the text around it: `A: ${t} ${?missing}` with no `missing` leaves `A: x`, without the trailing blank. The blanks at the start of a block line and the empty lines in between are content and are kept.
		* A value with no Slots is copied as it is: `Currency: EUR`.
		* Every Slot is required unless it carries a `?`: `?[list]`, `?{object}`, `?<var>` and `${?var}`. If a line had no `quantity` and the data had none further up either, or if `customer` were missing, fill would fail.
		* With no data item, `?[list]`, `?{object}` and `?<var>` remove the node, with its children. `${?var}` puts nothing, and the node still appears: `Phone: ${?phone}` would give an empty `Phone:`.
		* `Phone` does not come out because there is no `phone` either in the customer or in the root of the data. `Note` comes out in the first `Line` and not in the second. Only the navigation decides whether a node appears and how many times.
		* A `${?var}` with no data item only removes its own piece and the rest of the text stays: `Reference: ${number}${?revision}` produces `Reference: 2026-0142` if there is no `revision`.
		* An empty array produces no node and is not an error of fill. If the node was required, the validation of the filled document will say so.
		* A data item that does not fit its Slot is an error: an object where `<var>` or `${var}` expects a value, or a value where `[list]` expects an array.
		* The comment does not pass to the filled document.
		* A document with Slots is not validated. The one that is validated is always the filled document.
